From 0b61b6cf854530a424018df7ec0c03a912cd857d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shyalya <shyalya@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 14:41:03 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Embrace of the Viper: the five and six piece bonuses did not
 work

Reported as the five piece heal firing on every hit with no cooldown, and the
six piece bonus doing nothing whatsoever. Both were accurate.

Five pieces. Spell 44070 "Wild Regeneration Passive" carries
SPELL_AURA_PROC_TRIGGER_SPELL at procChance 100, with proc flags that include
taking any damage, and there was no spell_proc_event row for it at all. Its own
description states what was meant: "When your health drops below 35%, you rapidly
heal $44069o1 health over $44069d. This effect can trigger only once every 3
min." Neither half existed.

The cooldown is a spell_proc_event row. The health threshold cannot be expressed
there and comes as a spell script, and it has to account for the damage of the
hit proccing it: procs run first and damage second - Unit::AttackerStateUpdate
calls ProcDamageAndSpell and only then DealMeleeDamage - so on the blow that
takes you under the threshold your health is still the health you had before it.
Reading it as it stands refuses exactly the moment the bonus exists for.

That rules out OnCheckProc, which is not given the damage. Checking in OnProc
costs nothing: HandleProcTriggerSpellAuraProc tests the cooldown before casting
and only sets it afterwards, so a refusal never spends the three minutes.

Six pieces. Spell 44085 "Cat to Serpent Form" is a SPELL_AURA_DUMMY that no
handler ever looks at, so all six pieces changed nothing. Its text promises
something purely cosmetic: "Your Cat Form seems to react to the full embrace of
the Viper."

The machinery already exists - Player::GetShapeshiftDisplay keeps a table of
(form, aura, alliance model, horde model, defaults) that GetShapeshiftDisplayInfo
consults for FORM_CAT, and it is how every druid glyph form is done. Only the row
was missing. Display 4435 is "Cobrahn Snake Form", the serpent Lord Cobrahn takes
in Wailing Caverns: the Fang set drops there, its pieces are named after him, and
it is a druid's own shapeshift.

procFlags is left at 0 in spell_proc_event so the core keeps using the ones on
the spell itself - repeating them changes nothing and earns "field value
redundant" at startup.
